1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,064
Here, the state's piggy
bank is so full that each

2
00:00:03,104 --> 00:00:05,880
resident's share tops three
hundred thousand dollars.

3
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:09,928
Fish still dries on
wooden racks. In the wind.

4
00:00:09,968 --> 00:00:11,261
Like a thousand years ago.

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,723
Fjords more than a kilometre deep.

6
00:00:15,763 --> 00:00:17,946
Roads that hop from island to island.

7
00:00:17,986 --> 00:00:21,071
And sheep convinced those
roads were built for them.

8
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:32,102
Today we're flying to Norway.

9
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:41,480
Today it's one of the world's richest
countries. But it wasn't always so.

10
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:45,438
How did a nation of fishermen
fill such a piggy bank?

11
00:00:45,478 --> 00:00:49,189
And why do its millionaires still
take a packed sandwich to work?

12
00:00:49,229 --> 00:00:50,920
But first, the country itself.

13
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,228
Honestly, not the easiest place to live.

14
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:57,301
Look at the map.

15
00:00:57,341 --> 00:01:01,611
Norway stretches nearly one thousand eight
hundred kilometres along Europe's edge.

16
00:01:01,651 --> 00:01:05,342
Spin it around its southern tip, and
the north would almost reach Rome.

17
00:01:05,382 --> 00:01:08,601
The northernmost point hides a
catch. More on that at the end.

18
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:15,005
Count every fjord and island,
and the coastline runs

19
00:01:15,045 --> 00:01:17,128
about one hundred thousand kilometres.

20
00:01:17,168 --> 00:01:19,251
Two and a half times around the Earth.

21
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:24,035
All this is home to about
five and a half million

22
00:01:24,075 --> 00:01:26,233
people, roughly Scotland's population.

23
00:01:26,273 --> 00:01:28,200
But instead of streets, mountains.

24
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:29,820
Instead of bridges, ferries.

25
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:32,944
Instead of neighbours next
door, sheep over the fence.

26
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:38,828
One more oddity. Alaska and Siberia
lie at the same latitude as northern

27
00:01:38,868 --> 00:01:41,183
Norway, and their seas freeze in winter.

28
00:01:41,223 --> 00:01:45,244
Yet these ports stay open all year,
like nearby Murmansk, thanks to a

29
00:01:45,284 --> 00:01:48,364
warm Atlantic current that
continues the Gulf Stream.

30
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:53,502
Ice carved almost this entire country.

31
00:01:53,542 --> 00:01:58,262
For millennia, glaciers crept to the
sea, cutting deep valleys in the rock.

32
00:01:58,302 --> 00:02:01,816
When the ice melted, the sea
moved in. Fjords were born.

33
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:06,511
Norway's longest and deepest
fjord is the Sognefjord.

34
00:02:06,551 --> 00:02:09,362
Two hundred and five
kilometres long, one thousand

35
00:02:09,402 --> 00:02:11,414
three hundred and eight metres deep.

36
00:02:11,454 --> 00:02:15,461
Sink the Eiffel Tower there, and
almost a kilometre of water covers it.

37
00:02:17,590 --> 00:02:20,717
Here, waterfalls plunge straight
into the Geirangerfjord.

38
00:02:20,757 --> 00:02:23,551
The most famous even has
a name: the Seven Sisters.

39
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:27,492
Across from them falls the Suitor.

40
00:02:27,532 --> 00:02:31,335
Legend says he proposed to all
seven sisters. All seven refused.

41
00:02:31,375 --> 00:02:33,917
So he took to drink,
and, look closely, the

42
00:02:33,957 --> 00:02:36,379
waterfall really is shaped like a bottle.

43
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:43,714
But some Norwegian cliffs come with
balconies, not just waterfalls.

44
00:02:43,754 --> 00:02:45,606
Preikestolen, the Pulpit Rock.

45
00:02:45,646 --> 00:02:48,254
A flat stone platform above the Lysefjord.

46
00:02:48,294 --> 00:02:52,415
Below it, six hundred and four
metres of nothing. And no railings.

47
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:56,315
Hundreds of thousands of people
climb up here every year.

48
00:02:56,355 --> 00:02:59,206
Almost all take the same
photo: legs dangling over

49
00:02:59,246 --> 00:03:02,097
the abyss, face pretending
to be calm. Pretending.

50
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:08,102
Norwegians, meanwhile, sit
about three metres from the

51
00:03:08,142 --> 00:03:12,087
edge with a thermos of coffee and
a sandwich. Same view, almost.

52
00:03:12,127 --> 00:03:13,831
Home for dinner, guaranteed.

53
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:18,169
In the same Lysefjord there's an
even bolder spot. Kjeragbolten.

54
00:03:18,209 --> 00:03:21,037
A huge boulder wedged in a
crack between two cliffs,

55
00:03:21,077 --> 00:03:23,298
with almost a kilometre of nothing below.

56
00:03:23,338 --> 00:03:25,228
People step out onto it for photos.

57
00:03:25,268 --> 00:03:28,206
It has hung there for thousands
of years, in no hurry.

58
00:03:28,246 --> 00:03:30,467
Unlike the knees of whoever stands on it.

59
00:03:35,750 --> 00:03:38,821
This is Trolltunga, the
Troll's Tongue: a rock ledge

60
00:03:38,861 --> 00:03:41,215
about seven hundred metres above a lake.

61
00:03:41,255 --> 00:03:45,164
That photo costs a hike of over
twenty kilometres, there and back.

62
00:03:45,204 --> 00:03:46,839
A full day in the mountains.

63
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:51,886
And above the fjords, there's still ice.

64
00:03:51,926 --> 00:03:55,345
Jostedalsbreen, the largest
glacier on mainland Europe.

65
00:03:55,385 --> 00:03:59,622
Its tongues slide down into green
valleys, to the meadows and farms.

66
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:01,868
But the ice is retreating.

67
00:04:01,908 --> 00:04:06,099
In the late nineties, the Briksdalsbreen
glacier still reached the lake.

68
00:04:06,139 --> 00:04:10,095
Now it has pulled back up the slope,
leaving bare rock by the water.

69
00:04:10,135 --> 00:04:12,034
Locals are watching it disappear.

70
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:19,300
Further south, on the Hardangerfjord,
spring is another Norway altogether.

71
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:23,071
Apple and cherry trees bloom along
the water, white orchards run

72
00:04:23,111 --> 00:04:25,841
almost down to the waves,
and above them, snow.

73
00:04:25,881 --> 00:04:27,608
Spring and winter in one shot.

74
00:04:29,470 --> 00:04:32,931
On land like this, rock, ice
and a narrow strip of grass by

75
00:04:32,971 --> 00:04:35,602
the water, people lived
for a thousand years.

76
00:04:35,642 --> 00:04:38,035
Stubbornly, surviving however they could.

77
00:04:38,075 --> 00:04:40,349
But nobody would have called them rich.

78
00:04:53,310 --> 00:04:57,335
When people say 'a Norwegian from
the past', many picture a Viking.

79
00:04:57,375 --> 00:04:58,913
Beard, axe, horned helmet.

80
00:04:58,953 --> 00:05:02,493
Sorry to disappoint about the
helmet: Vikings had no horns.

81
00:05:02,533 --> 00:05:05,648
Nineteenth century artists
and theatre made them up.

82
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:08,485
The rest was real, though.

83
00:05:08,525 --> 00:05:11,831
In seven ninety-three, Vikings
raided Lindisfarne monastery

84
00:05:11,871 --> 00:05:14,441
in England, the usual
start date of their age.

85
00:05:14,481 --> 00:05:18,014
And around the year one thousand,
Leif Erikson reached America.

86
00:05:18,054 --> 00:05:20,396
Nearly five hundred years before Columbus.

87
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:24,715
But they didn't sail off
because life was good.

88
00:05:24,755 --> 00:05:27,152
Little land, short summers, long winters.

89
00:05:27,192 --> 00:05:30,482
And this land had just one
truly valuable product. Fish.

90
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:37,289
Then came Christianity, and Norwegians
built churches the way they built ships.

91
00:05:37,329 --> 00:05:41,281
Of wood, with almost no nails, the
frame held by wooden joints and pegs.

92
00:05:41,321 --> 00:05:42,944
And with dragons on the roofs.

93
00:05:42,984 --> 00:05:44,330
These are stave churches.

94
00:05:44,370 --> 00:05:47,323
Once there were over a
thousand. Twenty-eight survive.

95
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:54,121
Dried cod from the north
sailed south, to Bergen.

96
00:05:54,161 --> 00:05:55,570
Here's the Bryggen wharf.

97
00:05:55,610 --> 00:05:58,759
For centuries, German merchants
of the Hanseatic League

98
00:05:58,799 --> 00:06:01,542
traded here, bringing
grain and taking away cod.

99
00:06:01,582 --> 00:06:05,716
Bryggen burned more than once, and
each time it was rebuilt the old way.

100
00:06:09,010 --> 00:06:13,089
An old Bergen joke: no mountains in
sight? It's raining. Mountains visible?

101
00:06:13,129 --> 00:06:13,968
It soon will be.

102
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:20,351
In thirteen forty-nine, the plague
reached Bergen on a ship from England.

103
00:06:20,391 --> 00:06:25,237
Historians estimate about half of all
Norwegians died. Whole valleys emptied.

104
00:06:25,277 --> 00:06:27,457
No strength was left for a kingdom.

105
00:06:28,110 --> 00:06:31,463
Then Norway nearly vanished
as a country for centuries.

106
00:06:31,503 --> 00:06:34,425
Over four hundred years
of rule from Copenhagen.

107
00:06:34,465 --> 00:06:39,052
Officials spoke Danish, the capital
was far away, and taxes went there too.

108
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:44,735
In eighteen fourteen, Norwegians met at the
Eidsvoll manor and wrote a constitution.

109
00:06:44,775 --> 00:06:47,913
It was the seventeenth of May,
still Norway's biggest holiday.

110
00:06:47,953 --> 00:06:50,322
It's marked not with a
military parade but with

111
00:06:50,362 --> 00:06:52,680
children: school parades
with flags and bands.

112
00:06:56,370 --> 00:06:59,717
Many wear a bunad, each
valley's own national costume.

113
00:06:59,757 --> 00:07:04,171
A good one, with embroidery and
silver, can cost as much as a used car.

114
00:07:04,211 --> 00:07:06,367
It's worn for life and passed down.

115
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:12,609
A hundred and fifty years ago, Norway
was poor: fishermen and farmers.

116
00:07:12,649 --> 00:07:16,076
Over a century, some eight
hundred thousand left for America.

117
00:07:16,116 --> 00:07:18,577
In Europe, only Ireland
lost a bigger share.

118
00:07:21,370 --> 00:07:26,516
Whole families and villages left, because
there wasn't enough land or work at home.

119
00:07:26,556 --> 00:07:30,014
Today over four million
Americans claim Norwegian roots.

120
00:07:30,054 --> 00:07:32,576
Almost a second Norway, across the ocean.

121
00:07:35,510 --> 00:07:37,967
Those who stayed wanted
a country of their own.

122
00:07:38,007 --> 00:07:41,846
After the constitution, Norway spent
ninety years in a union with Sweden.

123
00:07:41,886 --> 00:07:44,237
Then, in nineteen oh
five, came a referendum.

124
00:07:44,277 --> 00:07:48,594
Three hundred sixty-eight thousand two
hundred and eight voted to leave the union.

125
00:07:48,634 --> 00:07:50,347
Against: one hundred eighty-four.

126
00:07:54,510 --> 00:07:57,091
One hundred eighty-four. They
probably knew them by sight.

127
00:07:58,810 --> 00:08:01,056
And the young country
made itself known at once.

128
00:08:01,096 --> 00:08:02,866
Not with war, but with skis and ships.

129
00:08:02,906 --> 00:08:05,580
Fridtjof Nansen had already
crossed Greenland on skis and

130
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:07,532
drifted through the ice on the ship Fram.

131
00:08:07,572 --> 00:08:09,866
And in nineteen eleven,
Roald Amundsen became the

132
00:08:09,906 --> 00:08:11,866
first person ever to reach the South Pole.

133
00:08:11,906 --> 00:08:14,295
A country that wasn't
independent six years earlier

134
00:08:14,335 --> 00:08:16,247
planted its flag at the end of the Earth.

135
00:08:27,690 --> 00:08:29,724
The twentieth century was no kinder.

136
00:08:29,764 --> 00:08:32,145
Germany occupied Norway in nineteen forty.

137
00:08:32,185 --> 00:08:36,063
In autumn forty-four, the retreating
Germans burned most of Finnmark

138
00:08:36,103 --> 00:08:39,002
and northern Troms, driving
people into the winter.

139
00:08:39,042 --> 00:08:42,344
Rebuilt almost from scratch,
the north has few old houses.

140
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:46,548
But that war also left a story
Norwegians are still proud of.

141
00:08:46,588 --> 00:08:49,847
A plant in the mountains made heavy water
for the Germans, for atomic research.

142
00:08:49,887 --> 00:08:53,439
In the winter of forty-three, a team of
Norwegian saboteurs on skis descended into the

143
00:08:53,479 --> 00:08:56,822
gorge, climbed a steep slope to the plant,
and blew up the heavy water equipment.

144
00:08:56,862 --> 00:08:59,871
Without a single shot. Their leader,
Joachim Rønneberg, was twenty-three.

145
00:08:59,911 --> 00:09:02,628
He lived to ninety-nine and always
said they were just very lucky.

146
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:17,382
Post-war Norway lived
modestly and worked hard.

147
00:09:17,422 --> 00:09:19,987
Nobody knew a new life
lay under the seabed.

148
00:09:21,610 --> 00:09:25,684
Then, in nineteen sixty-nine, a huge
oil field was found in the North Sea.

149
00:09:25,724 --> 00:09:27,797
And this is where it gets interesting.

150
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:34,357
It was named Ekofisk, and the government
heard the news just before Christmas.

151
00:09:36,550 --> 00:09:39,502
Quiet Stavanger, living
on canned fish and sprats,

152
00:09:39,542 --> 00:09:42,016
became the oil capital within a few years.

153
00:09:42,056 --> 00:09:46,804
Fishermen moved to the platforms, and
huge supply ships docked beside the boats.

154
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:50,388
Then Norway did something few expected.

155
00:09:50,428 --> 00:09:54,150
The oil was declared the whole
nation's wealth, not a few families'.

156
00:09:54,190 --> 00:09:56,640
The state took most of
the income and started

157
00:09:56,680 --> 00:09:58,908
thinking about grandchildren, not itself.

158
00:10:01,450 --> 00:10:04,438
No splurging: oil money goes
into a state fund, and the

159
00:10:04,478 --> 00:10:07,247
budget takes about three
percent a year on average.

160
00:10:07,287 --> 00:10:10,385
The fund now tops twenty
trillion kroner, about one and a

161
00:10:10,425 --> 00:10:12,698
half percent of the world's listed shares.

162
00:10:16,310 --> 00:10:19,621
Think of a granny with a pension
who spends very carefully.

163
00:10:19,661 --> 00:10:23,825
Except this granny owns a slice of
almost every big company on the planet.

164
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:28,170
A principled granny, too.

165
00:10:28,210 --> 00:10:32,488
The fund has an ethics council that can
recommend kicking a company out: for

166
00:10:32,528 --> 00:10:36,294
certain weapons, for tobacco, for
serious harm to people or nature.

167
00:10:36,334 --> 00:10:40,214
Landing on that list is like a bad
conduct mark from a whole country.

168
00:10:43,890 --> 00:10:47,716
Did you notice? Here, cod isn't eaten
right away either. It's hung up to dry.

169
00:10:47,756 --> 00:10:49,775
Remember that drying rack. It'll be back.

170
00:10:49,815 --> 00:10:53,892
And now hold on tight: we're diving off a
cliff. Enjoying the trip? Hit subscribe.

171
00:10:53,932 --> 00:10:56,855
Like the fund, we only take
three percent of your patience.

172
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:06,133
But we didn't come for the
money. We came for this.

173
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,277
Lofoten. Islands above the Arctic Circle,
where mountains rise straight out of

174
00:11:13,317 --> 00:11:16,432
the sea, and only about
twenty-five thousand people live.

175
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:28,480
These red cabins on
stilts are called rorbu.

176
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:32,066
Fishermen once lived in them
during the winter cod season.

177
00:11:32,106 --> 00:11:34,045
Now they're mostly for tourists.

178
00:11:34,085 --> 00:11:35,961
Who pay nothing like fishermen.

179
00:11:37,620 --> 00:11:40,871
Every winter the skrei, Arctic
cod, swims over a thousand

180
00:11:40,911 --> 00:11:43,873
kilometres from the Barents
Sea to spawn right here.

181
00:11:43,913 --> 00:11:47,106
And as a thousand years ago,
fishermen follow it to sea.

182
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:52,969
That's not laundry. That's cod.

183
00:11:53,009 --> 00:11:56,885
It dries in the wind from February
to June, with no salt and no fridge.

184
00:11:56,925 --> 00:11:59,477
It's been shipped to
Europe since Viking times.

185
00:12:02,810 --> 00:12:05,817
One of the biggest buyers
of dried cod is Italy,

186
00:12:05,857 --> 00:12:08,229
where it goes into traditional dishes.

187
00:12:08,269 --> 00:12:11,593
The dried heads go to Nigeria,
into soups and sauces.

188
00:12:11,633 --> 00:12:15,401
So a piece of Lofoten lands in
Italian and African kitchens.

189
00:12:17,950 --> 00:12:22,528
Henningsvær. A fishing village on islets
joined by bridges, with a football pitch

190
00:12:22,568 --> 00:12:25,949
among the rocks, where the sea
and mountains are the stands.

191
00:12:25,989 --> 00:12:30,339
On the dock, schoolkids cut out cod
tongues, a local delicacy, and sell them.

192
00:12:30,379 --> 00:12:33,247
Over a season, some earn
very serious pocket money.

193
00:12:36,900 --> 00:12:39,843
But let's be honest. Life
here isn't a postcard.

194
00:12:39,883 --> 00:12:43,012
In winter, the sun doesn't
rise for almost a month.

195
00:12:43,052 --> 00:12:47,921
Norway's prices are among Europe's highest:
about a third above the EU average.

196
00:12:51,270 --> 00:12:55,010
In summer, the opposite: for about
six weeks the sun never sets.

197
00:12:55,050 --> 00:12:58,259
So a home's most important
feature isn't the fireplace.

198
00:12:58,299 --> 00:12:59,735
It's very thick curtains.

199
00:13:01,330 --> 00:13:03,814
On Unstad beach, people in wetsuits surf.

200
00:13:03,854 --> 00:13:06,153
Above the Arctic Circle, in icy water.

201
00:13:07,410 --> 00:13:12,049
At the very end of the islands is a
village with the shortest name imaginable.

202
00:13:12,089 --> 00:13:15,888
Just one letter: Å. Written as
an 'A' with a little ring on top.

203
00:13:15,928 --> 00:13:17,808
Here the main Lofoten road ends.

204
00:13:17,848 --> 00:13:19,307
After that, only the sea.

205
00:13:23,210 --> 00:13:26,074
The roads are an attraction
too: sea bridges, hairpin

206
00:13:26,114 --> 00:13:28,595
bends, and views that
beg you to drive slowly.

207
00:13:30,670 --> 00:13:32,132
You'll drive slowly anyway.

208
00:13:32,172 --> 00:13:35,191
In summer, about two million
sheep roam free in Norway.

209
00:13:35,231 --> 00:13:38,084
And there's no point
negotiating the road with them.

210
00:13:43,550 --> 00:13:47,988
Eight kilometres, eight bridges, and
twelve hurricanes while it was being built.

211
00:13:48,028 --> 00:13:52,355
The Atlantic Ocean Road. It hops from
islet to islet right above the open sea.

212
00:13:52,395 --> 00:13:55,210
The builders apparently
decided a hurricane is just

213
00:13:55,250 --> 00:13:57,505
Norwegian weather, and calmly carried on.

214
00:14:00,090 --> 00:14:02,149
In storms, waves crash over the road.

215
00:14:02,189 --> 00:14:05,552
Believe it or not, people come
in bad weather just to watch.

216
00:14:08,470 --> 00:14:11,683
The main bridge, Storseisundet,
seems from one angle to end

217
00:14:11,723 --> 00:14:15,433
in mid-air: nicknamed the 'bridge
to nowhere'. It does go somewhere.

218
00:14:15,473 --> 00:14:17,914
It just pretends
beautifully that it doesn't.

219
00:14:21,260 --> 00:14:25,541
This is Trollstigen, the Trolls' Ladder:
eleven hairpins up a sheer slope.

220
00:14:25,581 --> 00:14:30,271
Built over eight years, it was opened in
nineteen thirty-six by the king himself.

221
00:14:32,550 --> 00:14:34,360
The Trolls' Ladder closes in winter.

222
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:35,850
Here, snow outranks the king.

223
00:14:37,250 --> 00:14:39,355
And this road sign isn't decoration.

224
00:14:39,395 --> 00:14:42,513
Moose are no metaphor:
hunters take tens of thousands

225
00:14:42,553 --> 00:14:44,777
a year, and sadly, cars hit many more.

226
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:50,485
Can't go around a mountain?
Norwegians go through.

227
00:14:50,525 --> 00:14:53,715
The Lærdal Tunnel: twenty-four
and a half kilometres under

228
00:14:53,755 --> 00:14:56,333
the mountains, the world's
longest road tunnel.

229
00:14:56,373 --> 00:14:58,004
About twenty minutes to drive.

230
00:14:59,930 --> 00:15:03,233
To keep drivers awake in the
monotonous dark, three huge

231
00:15:03,273 --> 00:15:06,099
caves were built inside,
lit in blue and yellow.

232
00:15:06,139 --> 00:15:08,786
As if dawn were breaking
inside the mountain.

233
00:15:08,826 --> 00:15:12,726
The only place in Norway with a
guaranteed sunrise all year round.

234
00:15:20,510 --> 00:15:24,700
Next door, a train crawls down to
the Aurlandsfjord. The Flåm Railway.

235
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:27,660
In twenty kilometres it
drops almost nine hundred

236
00:15:27,700 --> 00:15:29,775
metres, past waterfalls and cliffs.

237
00:15:29,815 --> 00:15:33,219
One of the steepest standard-gauge
railways in the world.

238
00:15:35,970 --> 00:15:38,154
Along the coast, since
eighteen ninety-three, runs

239
00:15:38,194 --> 00:15:39,666
the Hurtigruten, 'the fast route'.

240
00:15:39,706 --> 00:15:42,647
Its ships carried mail, people and
cargo to villages with no roads.

241
00:15:42,687 --> 00:15:45,404
In twenty eleven, TV showed an
entire voyage live: one hundred

242
00:15:45,444 --> 00:15:47,673
thirty-four hours non-stop,
just the passing coast.

243
00:15:47,713 --> 00:15:50,742
In one weekend, it was switched on
over two and a half million times.

244
00:15:50,782 --> 00:15:53,722
People watched a stranger's ship
window for days. And it was a hit.

245
00:16:02,890 --> 00:16:05,492
On the Sognefjord in
twenty fifteen, the world's

246
00:16:05,532 --> 00:16:07,693
first fully electric car ferry set sail.

247
00:16:07,733 --> 00:16:10,280
It charges at the dock
while cars drive aboard.

248
00:16:10,320 --> 00:16:13,637
It's so quiet that on deck you
can hear the seagulls arguing.

249
00:16:13,677 --> 00:16:16,718
But the most surprising thing
in Norway isn't the roads.

250
00:16:16,758 --> 00:16:18,424
It's how its millionaires live.

251
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:26,226
So, the country has a fund of
over twenty trillion kroner.

252
00:16:26,266 --> 00:16:29,874
You'd expect golden toilets,
limousines and a yacht at every pier.

253
00:16:29,914 --> 00:16:32,361
Let's see what wealth
really looks like here.

254
00:16:35,510 --> 00:16:37,044
This is Oslo, the capital.

255
00:16:37,084 --> 00:16:38,981
Hardly any show-off skyscrapers.

256
00:16:39,021 --> 00:16:42,067
Instead, an opera house
whose roof you can walk on.

257
00:16:42,107 --> 00:16:43,641
For free, almost all year.

258
00:16:43,681 --> 00:16:46,425
People sit there with
coffee, like on a beach.

259
00:16:48,890 --> 00:16:51,997
Every December, the Nobel
Peace Prize is awarded here.

260
00:16:52,037 --> 00:16:54,620
And Vigeland Park holds
more than two hundred

261
00:16:54,660 --> 00:16:57,418
sculptures of people: the
old, lovers, children.

262
00:16:57,458 --> 00:17:00,623
The most famous is a small
angry boy stamping his foot.

263
00:17:00,663 --> 00:17:03,945
Perhaps the only Norwegian
who doesn't hide his emotions.

264
00:17:06,230 --> 00:17:09,131
Norway is no longer just
fishermen and Vikings.

265
00:17:09,171 --> 00:17:12,384
Just over one in five
residents is an immigrant or a

266
00:17:12,424 --> 00:17:15,262
child of immigrants; in
Oslo, noticeably more.

267
00:17:15,302 --> 00:17:17,639
Its streets speak dozens of languages.

268
00:17:19,330 --> 00:17:20,867
Now look at the car parks.

269
00:17:20,907 --> 00:17:24,022
In twenty twenty-five,
ninety-six percent of all new

270
00:17:24,062 --> 00:17:26,691
passenger cars sold in
Norway were electric.

271
00:17:26,731 --> 00:17:31,059
Hardly anyone buys a new petrol car,
though old ones still drive around.

272
00:17:32,960 --> 00:17:34,751
So why is luxury so quiet here?

273
00:17:34,791 --> 00:17:38,237
Norwegians often explain it in
two words: the Law of Jante.

274
00:17:38,277 --> 00:17:41,959
In nineteen thirty-three, the
writer Aksel Sandemose invented a

275
00:17:41,999 --> 00:17:44,676
small town called Jante
and its unwritten law.

276
00:17:44,716 --> 00:17:48,221
The first rule goes: 'Don't
think you are anything special.'

277
00:17:52,780 --> 00:17:56,024
The writer was actually
mocking that law. But it stuck.

278
00:17:56,064 --> 00:18:00,742
So here you don't brag about your car,
your salary, or even running a marathon.

279
00:18:00,782 --> 00:18:03,071
Ran one? Well done. Just keep it quiet.

280
00:18:05,860 --> 00:18:07,268
The most unexpected part.

281
00:18:07,308 --> 00:18:09,931
In Norway, almost
everyone's income is public.

282
00:18:09,971 --> 00:18:12,000
Once a year, the tax office
publishes lists: what you

283
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:15,721
earned, how much tax you
paid and what you own.

284
00:18:15,761 --> 00:18:17,921
Almost anyone in the country can look.

285
00:18:21,140 --> 00:18:24,726
There's a catch, though. Since twenty
fourteen, people can see who looked them up.

286
00:18:24,766 --> 00:18:26,406
You can check your neighbour's salary.

287
00:18:26,446 --> 00:18:27,953
Then you have to keep saying hello.

288
00:18:32,580 --> 00:18:35,797
One thing Norwegians do show
off: the hytte, the cabin.

289
00:18:35,837 --> 00:18:40,298
Five and a half million people, over
four hundred and fifty thousand cabins.

290
00:18:40,338 --> 00:18:42,727
In mountains, by fjords, on tiny islands.

291
00:18:46,290 --> 00:18:50,402
Not palaces, though. A real old hytte
often has no internet, sometimes no

292
00:18:50,442 --> 00:18:52,506
running water, and an outdoor toilet.

293
00:18:52,546 --> 00:18:57,226
Well-off people spend weekends here chopping
wood and carrying water from a stream.

294
00:18:57,266 --> 00:18:58,933
Voluntarily. And they love it.

295
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:02,693
Firewood is a religion here.

296
00:19:02,733 --> 00:19:05,359
A neat woodpile by the house
is the same winter store as

297
00:19:05,399 --> 00:19:07,502
the cod on the racks,
only stacked with love.

298
00:19:07,542 --> 00:19:11,310
In twenty thirteen, the state TV behind
the Hurtigruten show aired a twelve-hour

299
00:19:11,350 --> 00:19:14,500
evening about firewood, eight hours
of it a fireplace burning live.

300
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:16,738
Almost a million people
tuned in at least once.

301
00:19:16,778 --> 00:19:19,832
Viewers seriously argued how to
stack logs: bark up or bark down.

302
00:19:29,130 --> 00:19:33,395
No cabin? No problem. Norway has a right
to roam: walk almost anywhere, even

303
00:19:33,435 --> 00:19:35,774
on private land, except yards and gardens.

304
00:19:35,814 --> 00:19:39,399
Pitch a tent in forests or
mountains, at least one hundred fifty

305
00:19:39,439 --> 00:19:42,572
metres from any house, for
up to two nights in one spot.

306
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:51,992
Imagine: your tent's by a lake on a
millionaire's land. He can't do a thing.

307
00:19:52,032 --> 00:19:53,584
And probably doesn't want to.

308
00:19:53,624 --> 00:19:55,890
He's at another lake right now. In a tent.

309
00:19:59,630 --> 00:20:02,677
The mountains are dotted with
hundreds of hiking cabins.

310
00:20:02,717 --> 00:20:07,198
Many open with one shared key that any
member of the trekking association can get.

311
00:20:07,238 --> 00:20:11,167
Some have a pantry: take what you
need, write it down, and pay yourself.

312
00:20:11,207 --> 00:20:12,986
No one watches you. Sounds naive?

313
00:20:13,026 --> 00:20:14,695
It has worked for many decades.

314
00:20:19,320 --> 00:20:23,230
There's even a word for it: friluftsliv,
'open-air life'. In any weather.

315
00:20:23,270 --> 00:20:26,746
Norwegians like to say there's no
bad weather, only bad clothing.

316
00:20:26,786 --> 00:20:30,263
In winter, babies here nap outdoors
in their prams, in the frost.

317
00:20:30,303 --> 00:20:32,860
And parents are sure they
sleep better that way.

318
00:20:45,980 --> 00:20:48,425
In winter, this love
of nature puts on skis.

319
00:20:48,465 --> 00:20:52,322
In all-time Winter Olympic medals,
Norway is number one in the world.

320
00:20:52,362 --> 00:20:55,090
A country with the population
of one big city has

321
00:20:55,130 --> 00:20:57,462
beaten America, Germany and everyone else.

322
00:20:57,502 --> 00:21:01,642
By the way, the English words 'ski'
and 'slalom' both come from Norwegian.

323
00:21:05,760 --> 00:21:07,827
Now, food. This is brown cheese, brunost.

324
00:21:07,867 --> 00:21:09,831
Brown, sweetish, a little like caramel.

325
00:21:09,871 --> 00:21:12,760
Its modern recipe was invented
in eighteen sixty-three by

326
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:15,175
seventeen-year-old Anne
Hov on a mountain farm.

327
00:21:15,215 --> 00:21:18,412
She added cream to the whey, and
the country is still grateful.

328
00:21:18,452 --> 00:21:20,775
It's sliced with a special
cheese slicer, also

329
00:21:20,815 --> 00:21:22,831
invented here, over a hundred years ago.

330
00:21:29,350 --> 00:21:32,170
At Easter, Norwegians read
crime novels. Seriously.

331
00:21:32,210 --> 00:21:34,974
In nineteen twenty-three,
a publisher advertised a

332
00:21:35,014 --> 00:21:37,050
new book on a newspaper's front page.

333
00:21:37,090 --> 00:21:41,705
The headline about a train robbery read so
much like real news, people believed it.

334
00:21:41,745 --> 00:21:45,350
Ever since, the Easter crime novel
has been a national tradition.

335
00:21:48,260 --> 00:21:49,826
Alcohol is stricter, though.

336
00:21:49,866 --> 00:21:54,299
Anything stronger than four point seven
percent is sold only by a state chain.

337
00:21:54,339 --> 00:21:58,944
On Sundays it's closed. It's how the state
has curbed drinking since Prohibition.

338
00:21:58,984 --> 00:22:01,410
So a party in Norway
is planned in advance.

339
00:22:01,450 --> 00:22:03,073
Roughly like a rocket launch.

340
00:22:06,600 --> 00:22:08,460
Here's the most surprising part.

341
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:12,972
The country with the world's biggest
piggy bank lives as if it had no money.

342
00:22:13,012 --> 00:22:17,306
Many even bring a boxed sandwich from
home to work. It's called matpakke.

343
00:22:17,346 --> 00:22:20,096
A millionaire with a
matpakke surprises nobody.

344
00:22:22,470 --> 00:22:24,047
And with the sandwich, coffee.

345
00:22:24,087 --> 00:22:27,012
Norwegians are among the
world's biggest coffee lovers.

346
00:22:27,052 --> 00:22:31,217
The thermos goes with them to the
mountains, fishing, skiing and to the cabin.

347
00:22:31,257 --> 00:22:33,912
But the real test of this
calm isn't at the cabin.

348
00:22:33,952 --> 00:22:36,230
It's in the north,
where winter has no day.

349
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:43,538
Tromsø, near the seventieth parallel.

350
00:22:43,578 --> 00:22:45,402
Many here truly love the dark.

351
00:22:45,442 --> 00:22:49,625
Above town rises the Arctic Cathedral,
like an ice floe on its edge.

352
00:22:51,480 --> 00:22:55,455
Late November to mid-January, the sun
doesn't rise here. Almost seven weeks.

353
00:22:55,495 --> 00:22:57,674
But it's not the black night many imagine.

354
00:22:57,714 --> 00:23:00,896
For a couple of hours a day,
the sky turns blue, pink, lilac.

355
00:23:00,936 --> 00:23:02,640
As if someone left the sunset on.

356
00:23:07,890 --> 00:23:09,370
And people don't hibernate.

357
00:23:09,410 --> 00:23:12,916
Candles in windows, skiing with
headlamps, coffee with friends.

358
00:23:12,956 --> 00:23:16,069
Researchers asked Norwegians
in the south, in Tromsø and

359
00:23:16,109 --> 00:23:18,264
on Svalbard how they feel about winter.

360
00:23:18,304 --> 00:23:21,754
The result: the further north,
the more calmly people take it.

361
00:23:24,910 --> 00:23:28,302
This is what the world flies
in for. The northern lights.

362
00:23:28,342 --> 00:23:31,434
Tromsø sits right under the
aurora belt, so on clear

363
00:23:31,474 --> 00:23:33,541
dark nights they show even in town.

364
00:23:36,110 --> 00:23:40,251
Locals look at them the way we look at
fireworks outside the window. Pretty, sure.

365
00:23:40,291 --> 00:23:41,577
But there's work tomorrow.

366
00:23:57,760 --> 00:24:02,676
Recent winters brought herring to the
fjords north of Tromsø, and whales followed.

367
00:24:02,716 --> 00:24:06,907
Orcas and humpbacks feed among the
islands in the blue polar twilight.

368
00:24:06,947 --> 00:24:10,654
In silent boats, you hear a huge
whale exhale beside the hull.

369
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:16,679
Further east, in Alta, people
thousands of years ago carved pictures

370
00:24:16,719 --> 00:24:20,277
into the shoreline rocks:
reindeer, boats, fishermen, bears.

371
00:24:20,317 --> 00:24:23,156
The oldest are more than
six thousand years old.

372
00:24:23,196 --> 00:24:26,755
So Norway's first 'photos' were
taken back in the Stone Age.

373
00:24:28,060 --> 00:24:31,671
Long before tourists, oil, even
Vikings, this was the home of the

374
00:24:31,711 --> 00:24:35,322
Sámi, the Indigenous people of
the North, for thousands of years,

375
00:24:35,362 --> 00:24:38,131
across today's Norway,
Sweden, Finland and Russia.

376
00:24:40,320 --> 00:24:43,191
No one knows exactly how
many Sámi live in Norway:

377
00:24:43,231 --> 00:24:45,171
Norway doesn't register ethnicity.

378
00:24:45,211 --> 00:24:47,326
Estimates: several tens of thousands.

379
00:24:47,366 --> 00:24:50,470
The Sámi have their own
language, flag and parliament.

380
00:24:50,510 --> 00:24:54,546
Across most of the country, reindeer
herding is their exclusive right.

381
00:24:56,030 --> 00:24:58,230
In spring, huge herds move from
winter pastures to the sea.

382
00:24:58,270 --> 00:24:59,938
Norway has over two
hundred thousand domestic

383
00:24:59,978 --> 00:25:01,722
reindeer, about two thirds
of them in Finnmark.

384
00:25:01,762 --> 00:25:04,189
Yet only around three thousand
people make a living from herding.

385
00:25:04,229 --> 00:25:06,581
Most Sámi are teachers, doctors,
engineers, like everyone else.

386
00:25:06,621 --> 00:25:09,542
And if you meet a reindeer on a northern
road, it's almost certainly not wild.

387
00:25:09,582 --> 00:25:10,757
It has an owner and an ear mark.

388
00:25:28,540 --> 00:25:29,753
Here we must be honest.

389
00:25:29,793 --> 00:25:33,732
For over a century, the state tried
to make the Sámi 'proper' Norwegians.

390
00:25:33,772 --> 00:25:37,547
Children went to boarding schools
where their own language was banned.

391
00:25:37,587 --> 00:25:41,144
For decades, land in Finnmark was
sold only to Norwegian speakers.

392
00:25:41,184 --> 00:25:43,106
Kvens and Forest Finns got the same.

393
00:25:43,146 --> 00:25:44,795
It was called Norwegianization.

394
00:25:48,780 --> 00:25:51,442
Many families stopped speaking
their language to their

395
00:25:51,482 --> 00:25:53,393
children, to make life easier for them.

396
00:25:53,433 --> 00:25:54,694
Languages began to vanish.

397
00:25:54,734 --> 00:25:58,446
In ninety-seven, the King of Norway
publicly apologised to the Sámi people.

398
00:25:58,486 --> 00:26:02,299
And in twenty twenty-three, the Truth
and Reconciliation Commission set up by

399
00:26:02,339 --> 00:26:06,202
parliament published a huge report: the
effects of that policy are still felt.

400
00:26:10,810 --> 00:26:14,534
Today Sámi is taught in schools
again, with news and songs in it.

401
00:26:14,574 --> 00:26:17,488
Yoik, the ancient Sámi
chant that missionaries once

402
00:26:17,528 --> 00:26:19,572
called sinful, now fills big stages.

403
00:26:19,612 --> 00:26:24,089
They say you don't yoik about someone,
you yoik them. Or a place. Or the wind.

404
00:26:27,940 --> 00:26:32,516
Now even further north. Between the
mainland and the North Pole lies Svalbard.

405
00:26:32,556 --> 00:26:36,777
Its main settlement, Longyearbyen:
about two and a half thousand people.

406
00:26:36,817 --> 00:26:40,682
Polar bears in the whole Barents
Sea: an estimated three thousand.

407
00:26:42,320 --> 00:26:44,140
The rule is simple. Leaving town?

408
00:26:44,180 --> 00:26:48,310
Take protection against bears, and the
governor openly recommends a rifle.

409
00:26:48,350 --> 00:26:50,846
But rifles aren't allowed
in shops and public

410
00:26:50,886 --> 00:26:53,157
buildings, and signs on the doors say so.

411
00:26:55,490 --> 00:26:58,571
Burials almost stopped
here by the mid twentieth

412
00:26:58,611 --> 00:27:01,562
century: bodies don't
decompose in permafrost.

413
00:27:01,602 --> 00:27:03,968
The seriously ill go to the mainland.

414
00:27:04,008 --> 00:27:07,154
As the joke goes, dying
is forbidden on Svalbard.

415
00:27:07,194 --> 00:27:08,974
It's not a law, just custom.

416
00:27:10,190 --> 00:27:13,823
In a mountain near town hides perhaps
the planet's most important pantry.

417
00:27:13,863 --> 00:27:17,646
The Global Seed Vault. Over a million
seed samples from around the world, in

418
00:27:17,686 --> 00:27:19,709
case disaster strikes somewhere on Earth.

419
00:27:19,749 --> 00:27:21,973
Norway stays true to
itself: keeping the most

420
00:27:22,013 --> 00:27:24,237
precious things in the
cold, for a rainy day.

421
00:27:24,277 --> 00:27:26,802
But this fairy tale, like
any other, has its price.

422
00:27:33,890 --> 00:27:34,997
We promised honesty.

423
00:27:35,037 --> 00:27:39,012
In one of the richest countries in
the world, almost one in four young

424
00:27:39,052 --> 00:27:42,281
people aged eighteen to
twenty-four say they feel lonely.

425
00:27:42,321 --> 00:27:44,002
And that's not the only price.

426
00:27:47,320 --> 00:27:48,377
First, the wallet.

427
00:27:48,417 --> 00:27:52,398
A coffee in an Oslo café: about
fifty kroner, almost five dollars.

428
00:27:52,438 --> 00:27:55,688
Half a litre of beer in a
bar: usually over a hundred.

429
00:27:55,728 --> 00:27:59,709
So a sandwich from home isn't
stinginess. It's financial literacy.

430
00:28:02,120 --> 00:28:02,971
Why so expensive?

431
00:28:03,011 --> 00:28:06,902
Wages are high, so any human labour is
expensive, from waiters to plumbers.

432
00:28:06,942 --> 00:28:07,478
Plus taxes.

433
00:28:07,518 --> 00:28:11,304
On most purchases, the state takes
twenty-five percent, on food, fifteen.

434
00:28:11,344 --> 00:28:15,130
And of every krone above a high salary,
almost half goes to the treasury.

435
00:28:15,170 --> 00:28:17,645
In return: free schools
and universities, nearly

436
00:28:17,685 --> 00:28:19,585
free healthcare and a decent pension.

437
00:28:26,850 --> 00:28:30,368
For Norwegians on Norwegian
salaries, it's normal life.

438
00:28:30,408 --> 00:28:34,314
But for a tourist, a student or
a migrant worker, it's tough.

439
00:28:34,354 --> 00:28:37,160
Many migrant workers,
mostly from Poland and

440
00:28:37,200 --> 00:28:40,135
Lithuania, live cramped
and count every krone.

441
00:28:41,950 --> 00:28:43,436
The second price is darkness.

442
00:28:43,476 --> 00:28:46,541
Yes, many in the north love
their winter. But not everyone.

443
00:28:46,581 --> 00:28:49,278
Doctors have long studied
the local winter insomnia:

444
00:28:49,318 --> 00:28:51,436
the body seems to lose track of the days.

445
00:28:51,476 --> 00:28:54,172
People fight back with
light: sun-like lamps at work

446
00:28:54,212 --> 00:28:56,120
and at home, candles, outdoor sports.

447
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:58,962
Others simply buy a ticket
south and wait for the sun.

448
00:29:05,330 --> 00:29:06,787
The third price is reserve.

449
00:29:06,827 --> 00:29:08,893
Norwegians are polite, but restrained.

450
00:29:08,933 --> 00:29:11,887
Strangers rarely start
conversations, and making local

451
00:29:11,927 --> 00:29:14,714
friends, newcomers say,
takes not months but years.

452
00:29:14,754 --> 00:29:17,707
They say you can spot a
Norwegian bus queue from afar:

453
00:29:17,747 --> 00:29:19,980
everyone stands a couple of metres apart.

454
00:29:22,510 --> 00:29:23,923
One more thing postcards skip.

455
00:29:23,963 --> 00:29:25,714
Small northern villages are emptying.

456
00:29:25,754 --> 00:29:29,491
Finnmark is larger than Denmark, yet
home to only about seventy-five thousand.

457
00:29:29,531 --> 00:29:32,250
Young people go south to study
and often don't come back.

458
00:29:32,290 --> 00:29:35,494
For those living and working in
the far north, the state offers tax

459
00:29:35,534 --> 00:29:37,915
breaks and writes off part
of their student loans.

460
00:29:37,955 --> 00:29:39,416
Still, people are hard to keep.

461
00:29:46,400 --> 00:29:51,335
Next, the oil paradox. Rivers and
waterfalls give Norwegians almost all their

462
00:29:51,375 --> 00:29:55,922
electricity: about ninety percent is
hydropower. The cars are electric.

463
00:29:55,962 --> 00:29:59,993
On the surface, the country is
a straight-A student in ecology.

464
00:30:00,590 --> 00:30:04,194
But oil and gas make up more than half
of everything Norway sells abroad.

465
00:30:04,234 --> 00:30:08,288
So clean living at home is largely paid
for by fuel that burns in other countries.

466
00:30:08,328 --> 00:30:10,984
Norwegians know this very
well and argue about it: how

467
00:30:11,024 --> 00:30:12,931
much longer to drill, and when to stop.

468
00:30:12,971 --> 00:30:15,527
After all, that very fund
depends on this money, and

469
00:30:15,567 --> 00:30:17,524
so do thousands of jobs along the coast.

470
00:30:27,710 --> 00:30:30,575
Even green energy is
sometimes paid for by others.

471
00:30:30,615 --> 00:30:34,410
In twenty twenty-one, the Supreme
Court ruled that Fosen peninsula

472
00:30:34,450 --> 00:30:37,431
wind turbines violate Sámi
reindeer herders' rights.

473
00:30:37,471 --> 00:30:42,312
Yet the turbines still turn: an agreement
with the herders took more than two years.

474
00:30:44,120 --> 00:30:48,923
Now, salmon. Almost half of all farmed
Atlantic salmon in the world is Norwegian.

475
00:30:48,963 --> 00:30:51,613
Those round pens in the
fjords are the farms.

476
00:30:54,100 --> 00:30:56,010
But the farms have serious problems.

477
00:30:56,050 --> 00:30:58,122
Salmon lice thrive in the crowded pens.

478
00:30:58,162 --> 00:31:00,993
The fish are treated for
them, often getting injured.

479
00:31:01,033 --> 00:31:04,621
In twenty twenty-four alone, almost
fifty-eight million salmon died

480
00:31:04,661 --> 00:31:07,438
in sea pens from disease,
injuries and other causes.

481
00:31:07,478 --> 00:31:09,875
That's about fifteen
percent of all the fish.

482
00:31:13,350 --> 00:31:16,849
Leftover feed and farm waste
settle on the fjord floor.

483
00:31:16,889 --> 00:31:19,037
Escaped fish mix with wild salmon.

484
00:31:19,077 --> 00:31:22,834
Scientists call this a main
threat to Norway's wild salmon.

485
00:31:22,874 --> 00:31:25,472
Rules are getting tougher, farms seek new

486
00:31:25,512 --> 00:31:28,304
technology, but the
debate is far from over.

487
00:31:30,440 --> 00:31:33,482
Another global debate: Norway
is one of the few countries

488
00:31:33,522 --> 00:31:35,753
where commercial whaling is still allowed.

489
00:31:35,793 --> 00:31:40,242
The quota for twenty twenty-six is one
thousand six hundred forty-one minke whales.

490
00:31:42,580 --> 00:31:46,742
The actual catch is far lower: about
four hundred whales a year recently.

491
00:31:46,782 --> 00:31:51,347
Officials say there are plenty of minke
whales and the species isn't endangered.

492
00:31:51,387 --> 00:31:55,837
Animal advocates reply that the hunt
itself is cruel. We won't decide for you.

493
00:31:55,877 --> 00:31:56,988
We show it as it is.

494
00:32:00,650 --> 00:32:02,188
And perhaps this is what matters here.

495
00:32:02,228 --> 00:32:04,098
In Norway, all of this
is discussed openly: in

496
00:32:04,138 --> 00:32:05,842
newspapers, in parliament, in the kitchen.

497
00:32:05,882 --> 00:32:09,080
It doesn't always work, or work fast,
but problems aren't swept under the rug.

498
00:32:09,120 --> 00:32:12,692
And after the darkest day since the Second
World War, the twenty-second of July, twenty

499
00:32:12,732 --> 00:32:16,138
eleven, when seventy-seven people were
killed, most of them very young, the country

500
00:32:16,178 --> 00:32:19,252
answered not with fear, but with the
words 'more democracy, more openness'.

501
00:32:32,040 --> 00:32:34,616
Our journey ends where
Europe ends. North Cape.

502
00:32:34,656 --> 00:32:38,679
A cliff three hundred and seven metres
high, and beyond it only cold sea.

503
00:32:38,719 --> 00:32:41,073
And the catch we promised
at the beginning.

504
00:32:44,090 --> 00:32:47,693
People come from all over the world to
stand at Europe's northernmost point.

505
00:32:47,733 --> 00:32:50,090
And almost no one knows
it isn't the northernmost.

506
00:32:50,130 --> 00:32:52,630
The neighbouring cape,
Knivskjellodden, is almost one

507
00:32:52,670 --> 00:32:54,356
and a half kilometres further north.

508
00:32:54,396 --> 00:32:57,280
But you have to hike there,
while here you can arrive by bus.

509
00:32:57,320 --> 00:32:59,101
So North Cape simply won on marketing.

510
00:33:19,070 --> 00:33:24,280
From the southern fjords to North Cape,
we've seen fjords over a kilometre deep, ice

511
00:33:24,320 --> 00:33:28,718
retreating before our eyes, roads
over the sea, auroras above rooftops.

512
00:33:28,758 --> 00:33:31,030
People living at the edge of the map.

513
00:33:32,770 --> 00:33:35,882
Fishermen chasing cod like
their great-grandfathers.

514
00:33:35,922 --> 00:33:38,064
Herders leading reindeer to the sea.

515
00:33:38,104 --> 00:33:41,277
Norwegians with a thermos
three metres from the edge.

516
00:33:41,317 --> 00:33:43,338
Sheep that never gave us the road.

517
00:33:43,890 --> 00:33:48,550
We came for the views. Honestly, the views
are so good your phone hand gets tired.

518
00:33:48,590 --> 00:33:50,671
But we're taking home more than that.

519
00:33:50,711 --> 00:33:54,683
One simple idea that Norwegians
somehow understood before many others.

520
00:33:56,960 --> 00:33:59,030
Norway grew rich in one generation.

521
00:33:59,070 --> 00:34:02,045
Its piggy bank is the
planet's biggest state fund.

522
00:34:02,085 --> 00:34:05,181
Yet it still lives almost
the way its fishermen did.

523
00:34:07,380 --> 00:34:10,401
A Lofoten fisherman never
spent his whole catch.

524
00:34:10,441 --> 00:34:14,292
Part he hung to dry in the wind,
for winter, for a rainy day.

525
00:34:14,332 --> 00:34:16,715
The oil fund is that same drying rack.

526
00:34:16,755 --> 00:34:19,585
Only it holds savings
for generations unborn.

527
00:34:22,020 --> 00:34:23,799
Norwegians remember being poor.

528
00:34:23,839 --> 00:34:26,851
They remember eight hundred
thousand people boarding

529
00:34:26,891 --> 00:34:29,551
steamships, for lack of
land and work at home.

530
00:34:29,591 --> 00:34:32,779
So wealth here isn't worn on
the wrist. It's put aside.

531
00:34:35,020 --> 00:34:36,249
What can such money even buy?

532
00:34:36,289 --> 00:34:38,305
A tunnel with a sunrise
inside a mountain: yes.

533
00:34:38,345 --> 00:34:39,705
Sun over Tromsø in December: no.

534
00:34:39,745 --> 00:34:41,192
A friend at the bus stop: also no.

535
00:34:41,232 --> 00:34:43,992
Maybe that's why Norwegians guard
what can't be sold: silence, a

536
00:34:44,032 --> 00:34:46,311
ski track, an evening by
the fire, a thermos for two.

537
00:34:46,351 --> 00:34:49,592
'Don't think you're better than us,'
says another rule of the Law of Jante.

538
00:34:49,632 --> 00:34:52,391
But their task now seems different:
making sure no one is alone.

539
00:35:06,020 --> 00:35:08,257
Winter will come. One
day the oil runs out.

540
00:35:08,297 --> 00:35:11,804
Maybe that's why one of the world's
richest countries still packs a

541
00:35:11,844 --> 00:35:15,616
sandwich for work, escapes to a cabin
without plumbing, and calls a rock

542
00:35:15,656 --> 00:35:19,375
above a fjord with a thermos of coffee
the perfect holiday. You know...

543
00:35:19,415 --> 00:35:20,487
we think we get them.

544
00:35:26,500 --> 00:35:30,023
Could you go seven weeks without
sun, for a summer when it never sets?

545
00:35:30,063 --> 00:35:31,957
Comment below. We really do read them.

546
00:35:35,520 --> 00:35:38,456
Norway dries cod and saves
for its grandchildren.

547
00:35:38,496 --> 00:35:42,646
In our next country, life happens
right on the street: at the market,

548
00:35:42,686 --> 00:35:46,107
on a tiny stool by the road,
with a bowl of soup at dawn.

549
00:35:46,147 --> 00:35:50,055
Heat, rice terraces and rivers of
motorbikes. Next stop: Vietnam.
